ed `import collections` inside `transform`. The logic itself is unchanged.\n\n**The rule.** The grid is split by divider lines into 4×4 panels.\n- The first panel of each panel-row holds a 2×2 square. That square's colour is the row's colour.\n- One other panel holds a pattern.\n- In the output, every panel shows that pattern at the same offset within the panel, recoloured to its row's colour.\n- The 2×2 squares and the original pattern colour disappear.\n\n**Building the output.**\n1. Clear every panel, leaving only the divider lines.\n2. For each panel-row, stamp the pattern mask into every panel in the row's colour.\n\n**Test input.** The pattern is the 8-cell colour-1 shape. It is stamped into all 25 panels, giving rows of colour 2, 3, 4, 6 and 7 from top to bottom.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    boxes = panels(g)\n    # group panels into panel-rows by their top coordinate\n    ys = sorted(set(b[0] for b in boxes))\n    rows = [sorted([b for b in boxes if b[0] == y], key=lambda b: b[1]) for y in ys]\n    # row colour = colour of the square in the first panel of each row\n    row_col = []\n    for r in rows:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = r[0]\n        sub = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n        vals = [int(v) for v in sub.ravel() if v != bg]\n        row_col.append(collections.Counter(vals).most_common(1)[0][0] if vals else bg)\n    # pattern = content of the one non-first panel that is not empty\n    mask = None\n    for r in rows:\n        for b in r[1:]:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = b\n            sub = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n            if np.any(sub != bg):\n                mask = sub != bg\n                break\n        if mask is not None:\n            break\n    out = g.copy()\n    for r in rows:\n        for (y0, x0, y1, x1) in r:\n            out[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Clear every panel, keeping only the divider lines.\")\n    if mask is None:\n        return out\n    for r, c in zip(rows, row_col):\n        for (y0, x0, y1, x1) in r:\n            h = min(mask.shape[0], y1 - y0 + 1)\n            w = min(mask.shape[1], x1 - x0 + 1)\n            reg = out[y0:y0+h, x0:x0+w]\n            reg[mask[:h, :w]] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Stamp the pattern in colour {c} into every panel of this panel-row.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The rule.** The grid is split by divider lines into 4×4 panels."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The first panel of each panel-row holds a 2×2 square."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- One other panel holds a pattern."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In the output, every panel shows that pattern at the same offset within the panel, recoloured to its row's colour."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each panel-row, stamp the pattern mask into every panel in the row's colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is stamped into all 25 panels, giving rows of colour 2, 3, 4, 6 and 7 from top to bottom."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the pattern in colour 4 into every panel of this panel-row."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"d89de4cd04296d5a89123f47184146917e9ecc382fe70f34117d7c58dd04b56b","task_sha256":"ff56cec356c67b8e981cb899543b9433700ab332959dd0c64dcb407de875af90","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
